Leigh’s account of Platonic *elenchus*—Socratic cross-examination—offers a useful contrast: questioning can bring cognitive self-knowledge into view, including recognition of what one does not know. The Caterpillar’s questioning resembles that method, but in a comic and unsettling distortion: it exposes Alice’s uncertainty without promising that a stable answer will emerge.

